Let clients name the shape of their cursor (the cursor-shape protocol,
`wp_cursor_shape_manager_v1`), so that whoever draws the cursor outside the picture is told
"text" or "pointer" instead of an image, and can show its own cursor of that shape. Offered
only while the cursor is told to somebody; otherwise nothing changes.

A patch against gst-wayland-display (https://github.com/games-on-whales/gst-wayland-display,
MIT License), commit 1301f86, on top of gst-wayland-display-render-on-commit.patch,
gst-wayland-display-cursor-messages.patch, gst-wayland-display-windows.patch and
gst-wayland-display-dialogs.patch in this directory, in that order. The lines of context are
from that project and from those patches.

It is kept small and in a shape that can be proposed to that project
(docs/adr/0009-host-compositor.md). There is no new property: it belongs to `cursor-messages`
(the second patch), and with that off no such global exists and nothing changes.

Why: with `cursor-messages` on, a client's cursor is told as the image the client hands in.
Whoever shows the picture on another machine would rather show that machine's own cursor
(its arrow, its I-beam, its hand), and an image does not say which one it is. Toolkits name
the shape where the compositor offers the protocol (GTK 4, for one), and hand in an image
where it does not.

What it changes:

- The compositor's state has an optional cursor-shape manager (Smithay's
  `CursorShapeManagerState`). It is made, and its global advertised, the first time a cursor
  sink is set (`State::set_cursor_sink`, which is what `cursor-messages` does). Until then
  there is no global. A compositor that draws the cursor into its frames has one image for
  every name, so offering the protocol there would turn every cursor into an arrow.
- Once made, the global stays when the sink is taken away again: clients keep what they have
  bound. A named shape is then drawn into the frames as every named cursor is (the built-in
  image).
- A new file, wayland-display-core/src/wayland/handlers/cursor_shape.rs, with
  `delegate_cursor_shape!(State)` and the empty `TabletSeatHandler` the delegate needs
  (the protocol also covers tablet tools; nothing here announces a tablet).
- Nothing else. Smithay calls `SeatHandler::cursor_image` with the name, and the second
  patch tells a named cursor by its name already (`wayland.cursor`, kind "named", `name`:
  the CSS name, as the protocol has it). Frame pacing, rendering, the hand-over of frames,
  input handling and the element are not touched.

Tests (`cargo test -p wayland-display-core`), four with a real Wayland client: shapes it
names are told by their names; the same shape is not told twice, and an image, a hidden
cursor and a name follow each other; with nobody told about the cursor there is no such
global among those the client hears of, and the compositor has not made one; the global is
made once and stays when nobody listens any more.

Known limits:

- A client that connected before the sink was set hears of the global later, as of any
  global that comes late; what it does then is up to its toolkit. With the property set
  before the element starts, the sink is set as the compositor begins, before a client can
  know its socket.
- The global is not taken away when `cursor-messages` is turned off again.
- Nothing is exposed through the C bindings.

Apply and build, in a checkout that has the first four patches applied:

    git apply <this file>
    cargo build --release -p gst-plugin-wayland-display -p wayland-display-core --features cuda

